# Requirement words

Use the following vocabulary:

| Word | Meaning |
|------|---------|
| MUST / REQUIRED / SHALL | Absolute; always do |
| MUST NOT / SHALL NOT | Absolute; never do |
| SHOULD / RECOMMENDED | Strong default; skip only with a valid documented reason |
| SHOULD NOT / NOT RECOMMENDED | Strong avoid; only if necessary |
| MAY / OPTIONAL | Truly optional |

Examples: MUST validate input. SHALL NOT share private user data. SHOULD use plain words. MAY suggest extras when useful.

# Disclose assumptions (MUST)

When your answer or work rests on something the user did not say, you MUST name
those beliefs so they can correct you. Faithful disclosure means listing every
**load-bearing** assumption — anything that would change your recommendation,
implementation, or scope if it were wrong.

**Grounded (do not list as assumptions):** explicit user instructions; text you
read this turn from the repo, a guide, a rule, a skill, or tool output; facts
followed directly from that evidence.

**Assumption (MUST list):** anything else you treated as true to proceed — guessed
intent, unstated preferences, default scope (“only this file”), environment (“they
use X”), priorities, naming or API choices not specified, interpretation of
ambiguous wording, or “typical” project behavior you did not verify in this
session.

**How:** If you made any load-bearing assumptions, end the user-facing reply with
a short **Assumptions** section: bullet list, one plain sentence per item. Omit
the section only when every decisive step was user-stated or evidenced above.
Question-only replies with a single obvious reading and no branching MAY omit the
section; if you chose among interpretations or filled gaps, you MUST include it.

Do not bury assumptions in prose; the section is the checklist the user scans.

# .dev-notes directory

If present, use for context. Do not invent policy. Do not bulk-read.

**Start:** `definition.md`, `vocabulary.md` (if present), `README.md` (if any),
`journal.md`.

**Vocabulary (MUST):** If `.dev-notes/vocabulary.md` exists, read it with the
startup set. Deliberately use its **Term** values (including multi-word phrases)
in user-facing prose, docs, and naming; think in that vocabulary instead of
generic synonyms unless the user overrides.

**As needed:**

| Path | When |
|------|------|
| `vocabulary.md` | Maintained via `build-vocabulary` skill (explicit invoke) |
| `knowledge/` | Detailed docs/notes |
| `artifacts/` | Stored artifacts (cited from docs) |
| `activities/` | `workon` context |
| `seed-prompt.md` | Recreate-from-scratch DNA (update only via `seed-prompt` skill) |

`journal.md`: append only via `journal` when asked. Guide create/rewrite/audit/delete:
`dev-guides` skill only (invoke on explicit request).

# Live tree wins

Repo source tree is source of truth. If a guide disagrees on specifics, trust the
tree (see "Keep guides fresh" below for your upkeep duty).

# Read guides (no `dev-guides` skill)

Open guides yourself — never invoke `dev-guides` only to read.

**Trigger (MUST):** Before you modify or add files under a project path `P`, read
the deepest matching `dev-guide.md` for `P` if one exists.
Trivial single-file lookup does not require this.

**Find for path `P`:** `<P>/dev-guide.md`, else walk up (`a/b/c` → `a/b` → `a`),
else repo-root `dev-guide.md`. Sparse is normal (intermediate folders may have no
guide). Do not auto-open root/siblings. Re-open when the subtree changes and needs
its guide.

**Use:** the summary, **Notes** (invariants, build/test, gotchas), and
**Artifacts** table. Repo-root guide uses the same shape; it does not
restate vision (see `definition.md` — link from Notes).

**Other guides:** When Notes or the artifact table names repo path `Q`, open
`<Q>/dev-guide.md` if you need that subtree. Otherwise walk up ancestors.

# Keep guides fresh (MUST)

If your change makes a `dev-guide.md` factually wrong — a renamed/moved/deleted
path in Notes or Artifacts, a changed build command, a stale table
row — you MUST fix it in the same change. For material drift (placement,
hierarchy, or what the guide is for), do not rewrite here: note it for the
`dev-guides` skill.
